 scores highest for soil, so it carries your soil score.","evidence":"Alfalfa is the thirstiest common crop in central Arizona, often 5 to 6 acre-feet per acre per year. It is a legume, so it adds its own nitrogen.","benefits":"Steady hay income, living roots all year, nitrogen for the next crop, and almost no tillage once planted.","gaps":"We model alfalfa as a 4-year stand averaged per year. We do not model stand decline, pests, or hay quality discounts.","checkLocally":"Ask about your stand age, hay contracts, and whether deficit irrigation in summer makes sense on your soil."},{"id":"cotton_barley","name":"Cotton then barley","seasons":["Cotton: Apr to Oct","Barley: Dec to Mar"],"calendar":["grow","grow","grow","grow","grow","grow","grow","grow","grow","grow","bare","grow"],"heatRiskMonths":[6,7,8],"soil":{"legume":0,"livingRoots":14,"residue":13,"lowTillage":10,"diversity":18,"total":55},"stats":{"avgProfit":500,"profitPerAf":113.64,"worst1in5":99.54,"avgWaterAf":4.4},"why":"Takes 60% of your acres in the suggested plan. It earns well in good price years and uses less water than alfalfa.","evidence":"Upland cotton in Pinal County typically uses around 4 acre-feet per acre. Winter barley uses much less because it grows in cool months.","benefits":"Two crops a year, a winter cover of living roots, and residue that protects the soil.","gaps":"Cotton prices and yields vary a lot. We use 20 past years of prices, which may not match the next 20.","checkLocally":"Check gin access, pink bollworm history, and whether your barley can be sold or grazed nearby."},{"id":"durum_sorghum","name":"Durum wheat then sorghum","seasons":["Durum wheat: Dec to May","Sorghum: Jul to Oct"],"calendar":["grow","grow","grow","grow","grow","bare","grow","grow","grow","grow","bare","grow"],"heatRiskMonths":[6,7,8],"soil":{"legume":0,"livingRoots":15,"residue":17,"lowTillage":10,"diversity":18,"total":60},"stats":{"avgProfit":300,"profitPerAf":83.33,"worst1in5":155.67,"avgWaterAf":3.6},"why"